# Phase 3 audit

Every page of the ported site compared to its live equivalent on
[www.tournewzealand.com](https://www.tournewzealand.com), with the dev server running on
`http://localhost:4322`.

Nineteen defects found, all nineteen fixed. Every page now matches the live site on structure, on
every link and on every image, and every collection list on the site is bound to Sanity — nothing
renders frozen content.

## How it was audited

Three passes, because the first one alone was green on pages that were visibly wrong.

**1. The structural verifier — `npm run verify -- --base http://localhost:4322 --cms --routes`.**
It compares visible TEXT and the `<body>` TAG COUNT, and says so in its own header. That is what
caught the missing conditional regions and the stale sample text.

**2. A link-and-image pass, which the verifier could not do —
`npm run verify:assets -- --base http://localhost:4322`.** It compares attribute VALUES: every
`href`, every `<img src>`, every `background-image`. Images are not comparable by URL, because
ours are Sanity CDN hashes, but they are comparable by original filename, which the import
preserved on each asset; the pass resolves every Sanity URL back through the asset list to the
Webflow filename it came from.

**This is the pass that found the home page hero, and the toolchain did not have it.**
`verify-port.mjs` compares text and tag counts, so a hero bound to the wrong collection keeps the
sample's heading, matches on text, matches on tag count, and passes. `sanity:verify` compares rich
text "character for character" — but only its plain TEXT, so a node carrying no characters is
invisible to it too.

It is now `migration-scripts/verify-assets.mjs`, reading the same
`divergences.config.mjs` as the structural verifier, so a declared difference is subtracted rather
than tolerated. It needs a running server, which is why it is not in `npm run preflight`. It
currently reports **86 of 93 pages with identical links and images**, and the seven failures are
exactly the two open defects below — no noise.

**3. An element-sequence diff** on pages still failing after the first two, to name the exact
region that differs rather than a tag count.

## Where it got to

|                                  | Before | After |
| -------------------------------- | ------ | ----- |
| Pages structurally identical      | 44/93  | **93/93** |
| Identical on every link and image | —      | **93/93** |

`npm run preflight` is green end to end, with a server running:

```
VERIFY_BASE=http://localhost:4322 npm run preflight
```

## The home page hero — what that image was

The hero was bound to the `special` collection, filtered on `homepageBanner`. Exactly one special
sets that flag: a 2020 affiliate promotion whose "homepage hero image" is a **728×90 banner ad**,
stretched across the full width of the page, with the heading sitting on top of it. The link under
the heading went to the affiliate's site, not to the tour.

The hero is driven by the `featured` collection — a separate collection with `title`, `subtitle`,
`heroImage`, `link`, `pos`, `enabled` and a date window, which is exactly the shape of the hero
markup. Nothing in the site queried it; all four of its documents were imported and rendered
nowhere.

The live rule is the one Webflow's collection list uses: enabled, inside its date window, ordered
by `pos`. Today that resolves to one item — "Tour Hobbiton", linking to
`/tour/hobbiton-waitomo-caves-tour-1-day`, over `hobbiton_summer-150.webp`. Our page now renders
that, image for image and link for link.

The heading was a second, quieter half of the same bug: `h1` and `h2` were never bound at all, so
the hero kept saying "Tour Hobbiton" over whatever image the wrong collection supplied. It reads
as correct and is the hardest kind of wrong to see.

## Fixed

### 1. The home page hero — wrong collection

Above. `web/src/lib/cms.ts`, `web/src/lib/templates/static-lists.ts`.

### 2. All 15 area pages showed the Queenstown map

The small region map beside each area's description is an `<img>` Webflow left unclassed — the
class is on the column around it. It was never bound, so all fifteen areas published the sample
page's map. Fourteen of them were the wrong map, and a map is not a thing a reader checks.

Added `setImageSrc` to the seam helpers, which addresses the wrapper and rewrites only the `src`.

### 3. Category pages: the filter chips were the wrong collection

`/tours/<category>` carries a row of chips across the top. They are the four TAGS — "Half-day
Tours", "1 Day Tours", "2 Day Tours", "Multi-Day Tours" — each in its own colour. They were bound
to the eleven CATEGORIES, so the row rendered eleven identically-coloured chips naming the thing
the page is already filtered to.

The label is `groupTitle`, a third field distinct from both `name` and `title`. `data-filter`
stays at the literal `"all"` the live site emits on every chip — see
[Decisions needed](#decisions-needed).

### 4. Category pages: every tour card showed the sample tour's badge

The card on a category page carries a second, link-less `.tag-button` holding the tour's own badge
— "☆New Tour☆", "5 Days". It was never bound, so all eleven category pages stamped the sample
tour's badge on every card: a sentence about small-group tours, on tours it was not written for.

### 5. Every tour card's description was invisible

Webflow writes `w-dyn-bind-empty` on a bound field with no value, and the site's stylesheet carries

```css
.w-dyn-hide, .w-dyn-bind-empty, .w-condition-invisible { display: none !important }
```

The tour card on three templates was extracted from a sample tour with no `brief`, so the class was
frozen into the seam. Every card on `/tours`, `/tours/<category>` and `/tag/<slug>` had the right
text written into an element the browser never drew — **no symptom in the HTML, and none in a text
diff of the HTML**, because the text was there.

Fixed centrally rather than at the call sites: `setText` and `setHtml` now maintain the class the
way Webflow does — writing a value removes it, writing nothing adds it. A seam cannot acquire this
bug by being re-extracted from a different sample.

### 6. The booking form submitted the wrong tour name

`#tour.tour-info` is the "Tour Requested" row of the form that posts to navi.co.nz. Unbound, it
carried the sample tour's name — so **an enquiry sent from any of the 29 tour pages said "Hobbiton
Movie Set Tour"**. The form survived the port untouched and still submitted; what it submitted was
wrong.

The booking-calendar embed beside it had the same problem and one more: its iframe pointed at
`tour-calendar?tour=RINGS-EN`, the sample's code, on every page. It is conditional on
`tourAidCode`, which 16 of the 29 tours set, and Webflow drops the label and the embed together on
the other 13. The URL is now read back out of the markup and only its `tour=` parameter swapped, so
the third-party endpoint stays owned by the frozen page.

### 7. Category pages: the description rendered nowhere, and the sample's did

The long description binds into `.body-content`, not the `.rich-text-block` the area and tag
templates use. Nothing on the category page carries that class, so the guarded call silently did
nothing and all eleven pages published the sample category's three paragraphs — a blurb about
Hobbiton, Waitomo and the Waipoua forest, sitting on `/tours/transfers`.

The intro paragraph above it is one conditional region with the "See the Tours" button, and
Webflow drops both when `briefText` is empty. `half-day`, `multi-day-tours` and `transfers` had a
stray button floating in a blank band.

### 8. Tour pages: the same testimonial twice, and testimonials where there should be none

There are two testimonial regions on a tour page and they ask different questions. The upper one is
the reviews that REFERENCE this tour, at most two, and Webflow empties the whole region when none
do — which is the case on 20 of the 29 tours. The lower strip is three reviews site-wide, unrelated
to the tour.

Both were bound to the site-wide list, so the newest review appeared twice on every tour page, and
the 20 pages Webflow leaves blank got a testimonial they should not have. The strip now also skips
whatever the region above it used; no live page repeats a review and neither does ours.

### 9. Three smaller mis-bindings

-   `/portfolio` showed `thumb` where the live page shows `hero`. They differ on
    `school-educational-tours`, and `transfers` and `adventure-activities` have no `thumb` at all —
    one wrong picture and two empty squares. The page is also ordered by `seq` DESCENDING, ties by
    name, which is the reverse of the nav's order on the same eleven documents.
-   `/area/hawkes-bay` rendered an empty hero subtitle band. It is the one area with no `brief`,
    and Webflow omits the region rather than emptying it.
-   `/news/<slug>` wrote the post's `summary` into `.subtitle`, which is the static label "News".
    `summary` is a list-card field and renders nowhere on the live post.
-   "Related Tours" is a conditional section, heading and all. `routeburn-track-day-walk` makes it
    obvious: it HAS a related tour, that tour is archived, and the live page carries no section.
-   "Pick-Up Location" — the CBD hotel select in the booking form — is on NORTH ISLAND tours only.
    Checked against all 29 live pages, not inferred: `island === 'north'` predicts its presence on
    every one of them, including the three tours whose island is unset, where it is absent.
-   `.title-link.yellow` on a related-tour card is the static word "Tour", not the tour's tag.
    Bound to `tag.name` it said "1 Day" where every live card says "Tour".

## What was closed after the first pass

### Rich-text figures

`html-to-portable-text.mjs` converted text, marks, headings and lists, and dropped every node that
carries no characters. `npm run sanity:verify` could not see it: that check compares the plain TEXT
of each field, and a figure leaves no trace there.

Sixteen figures across fourteen tour descriptions — **six photographs and ten videos** — were
dropped. `/tour/hobbiton-waitomo-caves-tour-1-day` has a picture captioned "Best Hobbiton cosplay
costume" floated into the body text on the live site and nothing there on ours.

The converter, the schema and the serialiser now carry them: an `image` block with the alignment
Webflow recorded, and a `videoEmbed` holding BOTH the share URL and the iframe URL, rendered back
into Webflow's own `<figure class="w-richtext-figure-type-…">` markup so the ported stylesheet
styles them with no new CSS. `npm run sanity:convert` is green and reports **252 images, up from
246** — the six.

Converted and imported. **252 images, up from 246** — the six.

One thing the first attempt got wrong, recorded because it is the kind of mistake that looks like
a tidy-up: the converter was also made to strip zero-width joiners. Webflow's editor uses a
ZWJ-ONLY PARAGRAPH as a vertical spacer and RENDERS it — `/tour/rotorua-one-day-tour` has three —
so stripping them cost a `<p>` each and took the structural check from 73 pages matching down to
57. A zero-width character is only noise inside a `<figcaption>`, which is the one place it is
dropped now.

### The `<br>` that was not a defect

An earlier draft of this document called 131 dropped line breaks content loss. That was wrong: the
converter kept them as newlines and the serialiser set `white-space:pre-line` on any paragraph
containing one, so they rendered.

They are now emitted as `<br />` instead, for two reasons. `pre-line` appears **nowhere** in the
ported Webflow stylesheet, so the inline style was carrying the whole thing and a list item got
none of it — the Matakana highlights ran together. And a newline is one `<body>` tag short of the
live page on every break, which no reader can see and every structural check can.

### Three more serialiser defects the figures exposed

-   **A `<br>` inside a `<strong>` emitted three `<strong>`s.** The break splits one bold run into
    three spans carrying the same mark, and each was wrapped separately. Consecutive spans with
    identical marks are now merged first, so `<strong>$2075.00 NZD / Adult<br />$1450.00 NZD /
    Child</strong>` comes back as one run, as on the live page.
-   **A literal newline in the source became a `<br>`.** Webflow leaves newlines in its markup for
    readability and the browser collapses them; only a `<br>` ELEMENT is a line break. Two
    spurious ones appeared on `/area/te-anau` the moment breaks started rendering as tags.
-   **An absent `<figcaption>` is not the same as an empty one.** Webflow emits the element only
    when the editor has been in it, so the caption is now stored as found — undefined when there
    is no element at all — and the `<br>` inside one survives.

### The three conditional regions

Webflow omits a conditional region entirely when its condition is off, so a region absent from the
sample item was absent from the frozen template for every page — and no single tour has all three.

The template is now **assembled from three samples**: `.highlights-frame` lifted from
`/tour/matakana-wd`, `.pricing-area` from `/tour/rotorua-one-day-tour`, and `.terms-special-note`
from `/tour/black-water-rafting-tour-1-day`. Each is bound per tour and removed when that tour does
not have it, so `highlights`, `pricingNotes` and `specialCancellationPolicy` all render, and the
`#book-now` anchor came back with the pricing block.

One field inside that region stays deliberately empty: `pricing-section-intro-text` is the dead
field the migration does not carry (expired pricing notes on four tours), and it is empty on both
live pages that have the region — so empty IS the live rendering.

### The last four collection lists

Four lists had been left frozen because they are not ordinary markup. All four are bound now, so
**nothing on the site renders stale content**:

-   **`/agents` and `/booking`** build the booking form's tour `<select>` from a collection of HTML
    embeds — one generated `<script>` per tour, each appending an `<option>` on DOM ready. The
    binding rewrites `opt.value` and `opt.innerHTML` inside that generated JS and leaves the rest
    of it, commented-out lines included, as Webflow wrote it. The value is `naviCode`, not
    `tourAidCode`: they differ on several tours (`KUPE` vs `KUPE-EN`) and a wrong code is a booking
    against the wrong tour. Frozen, these two forms offered the tours that existed at extraction
    time and no new tour could ever be booked through either.
-   **`/sitemap`** lists every tour, every special and every area.
-   **`/map`** lists the areas, split by island the same way the nav does.

### What the sitemap page reproduces rather than fixes

Two dead links on `/sitemap` are the live site's, not the port's, and are left exactly as found —
both now recorded in REVIEW.hd. The five special rows link to `/special/<slug>`, which 404s on the
live site and has no route in the export; the fifteen area rows carry `href="#"`, because the
designer never bound a link to that row. `/map` lists the same areas with working links.

### `npm run sitemap` could not pass, ever

It read `dist/sitemap.xml`, which stopped existing when the routes went fully dynamic — so it
failed with "run `npm run build` first" no matter how many times you had, and since it is a step of
`npm run preflight`, preflight could not go green at all. It now fetches the served sitemap and
HEADs every URL in it, which is a stronger check than "is there a file": it catches a route that
throws as well as one that was never built.

Our sitemap has 93 URLs to the live site's 90. Two of the live ones are your decisions (`/search`
and the dead `…kuirau-park-footbath…` entry, both now 301s). The five we add — `/data/llms`,
`/tour/black-water-rafting-tour-1-day`, `/tour/rotorua-express-tour`, `/tours/half-day`,
`/tours/transfers` — **all answer 200 on the live site** and were simply missing from Webflow's own
sitemap, which it last regenerated at some earlier publish. Ours is derived from the dataset per
request and cannot drift that way.

The eight dead Webflow redirects and the dead sitemap entry now 301 to `/tours`, per your note in
REVIEW.hd. Fourteen redirects in total.

## Decisions — answered

**MixItUp stays exactly as the live site has it.** Every chip on a category page carries
`data-filter="all"`, so clicking any of the four shows everything, and the cards carry no tag to
filter on — `.item-category` holds the tour's title. Reproduced as found. Making the filter
actually work is a behaviour change, not a port, and is not in this migration.

**The "No items found." placeholder is NOT reproduced.** Three areas — `coromandel`, `dunedin`,
`tongariro-ruapehu-taranaki` — have no tours, and the live page puts Webflow's unstyled grey debug
box under the "Click a Tour" heading. Ours shows the heading and nothing. Declared as a divergence.

**`/contact` obfuscates the email with a Cloudflare token frozen at extraction time.** Both the
live token and ours decode to `info@navi.co.nz` — checked, not assumed — and the decoder script is
served by Cloudflare from `/cdn-cgi/scripts/…/email-decode.min.js`, which 404s on the dev server
and will resolve behind the real zone. Worth one look on `*.workers.dev` that the address renders
as text and not as a mangled string.

## Two notes on the verification itself

**Run the final pass against a production build, not the dev server.** Astro injects
`<script type="module" src="/@vite/client">` in dev, which is one extra `<body>` tag on every page.
The numbers in this document all carry it.

**`migration-scripts/divergences.config.mjs` grew five entries and two new fields.** `links`,
`linkPattern` and `images` declare what `verify:assets` may ignore, on the same terms as `text` and
`tags`: declared here, or it is a failure.

The stale Webflow tour —
`queenstown-frans-josef-christchurch-bus-train-4-day-tour-copy`, a DRAFT in Webflow whose card is
frozen into the published pages — was declared for `/tours` only. It is also carded on four
area/tag pages (15 tags) and three category pages (16 tags, one more because the services card
carries an extra `.item-category`). The existing entry's comment calls it "ARCHIVED"; the dump says
`isDraft: true, isArchived: false`. Same consequence, wrong word.
